    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q1-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The table below shows the maximum daytime temperature, in C ° , at a certain city centre, and the amount of a certain pollutant in mg per litre. Maximum Temperature 10 12 14 16 18 20 22 24 Amount of Pollutant 513 475 525 530 516 520 507 521 a) Find, using a statistical calculator, the value of the product moment correlation coefficient for the above data. ( ) 1 b) State, with justification, the value of the product moment correlation coefficient, if the maximum daily temperatures were to be measured in degrees Fahrenheit. ( ) 2\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q1-question-2.svg\" alt=\"c) Test, at the 10% level of significance, whether there is evidence of positive correlation in these bivariate data. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:578.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q1-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Enter the eight temperature and sales pairs in corresponding rows of a two-variable calculator: Calculate the PMCC Sxy/ pSxxSyy. The sample product moment correlation coefficient is r ≈0.3199 . [A1] (b) The Fahrenheit conversion is a positive linear rescaling, F = 9 5C + 32. [B1] It leaves r unchanged: [A1] (c) Setting hypotheses: Test H0 : ρ = 0 against H1 : ρ &gt; 0, [B1] where ρ is the population PMCC: [A1] With n = 8, the one-tailed 10% critical value is 0.5067. As 0.3199 &lt; 0.5067, [A1] fail to reject H0. There is insufficient evidence of positive correlation at 10% . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 1 is 7 marks.</p>"
