    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q4-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The events A and B are such so that 17 19 P ( ) A = P ( ) B ,     ( P A ′ ∩ B ′ ) = ,      ( P A ′ ∪ B ′ ) = . 24 24 Find the value of ( P A ∩ B ) . ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:574.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q4-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Since P(A) = P(B), the complements have equal probabilities: Applying the addition rule to the complements gives P(A′ ∪B′) = P(A′) + P(B′) −P(A′ ∩B′). [M1] Thus 19/24 = 2 P(A′) −17/24, [A1] so 2 P(A′) = 36/24 and P(A′) = 3/4. Hence P(A) = P(B) = 1/4 . [A1] The complement of A′ ∩B′ is A ∪B, so P(A ∪B) = 1 −17/24 . [A1] = 7/24 Equivalently, De Morgan’s law applied to the other given union gives P(A ∩B) = 1 −P(A′ ∪B′) = 1 −19/24 = 5/24 directly:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Now apply the addition rule to A and B. This gives 7/24 = 1/4 + 1/4 −P(A ∩B), [A1] hence P(A ∩B) = 5/24 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The events A and B are such so that 17 19 P ( ) A = P ( ) B ,     ( P A ′ ∩ B ′ ) = ,      ( P A ′ ∪ B ′ ) = . 24 24 Find the value of ( P A ∩ B ) . ( ) 6"
