    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-j/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The times, in minutes, taken by Year 6 students to complete the SATS Science test are assumed to be Normally distributed with mean of 48 and standard deviation of 5. 8% of the students finished the exam in less than t minutes. a) Find the value of t , correct to the nearest minute. ( ) 4 b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student took more than 57 ( ) minutes to complete the test. 4 20 students that sat the SATS Science test are selected at random. c) Calculate, correct to 3 significant figures, the probability that more than 2 of these 20 students took more than 57  minutes to complete the test. ( ) 4\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"It is claimed that the &quot;top set students&quot; take less time to complete the exam as the mean finishing time of a random sample of 6 &quot;top set students&quot; was 44 minutes. d) Test this claim at the 1% level of significance. ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:573.3px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Putting the information in a diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:546.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Let X ∼N(48, 52) be the finishing time: (a) P(X &lt; t) = 0.08 [B1] implies t = 48 + 5Φ−1(0.08) ≈40.9746, [A1] or 41 minutes . [M1] [A1] (b) P(X &gt; 57) = 1 −Φ(1.8) ≈0.03593 . [M1] Standardise 57 [A1] obtain 1.8 [M1] use the upper tail [A1] evaluate (c) Let Y ∼B(20, p), where p = 1 −Φ(1.8). [B1] [A1] The complement consists of exactly zero, one or two: (1 −p)20 + 20p(1 −p)19 + 190p2(1 −p)18. The probability is 2 20 ! pk(1 −p)20−k ≈0.0335 . X P(Y &gt; 2) = 1 − [M1] [A1] k k=0 (d) Setting up hypotheses:\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"Let µ denote the mean top-set finishing time: [M1] Test H0 : µ = 48 against H1 : µ &lt; 48, [A1] using the standard deviation 5. [A1] The sample gives z = 44 −48 √ ≈−1.9596. [A1] 5/ 6 Using the critical-value approach, the 1% lower critical value is −2.3263. The observed statistic −1.9596 is above this cutoff: The lower-tail p-value is 0.0250, greater than 0.01. Fail to reject H0. [A1] There is insufficient evidence at 1% that top-set students finish faster: [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 18 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The times, in minutes, taken by Year 6 students to complete the SATS Science test are assumed to be Normally distributed with mean of 48 and standard deviation of 5. 8% of the students finished the exam in less than t minutes. a) Find the value of t , correct to the nearest minute. ( ) 4 b) Determine the probability that a randomly chosen student took more than 57 ( ) minutes to complete the test. 4 20 students that sat the SATS Science test are selected at random. c) Calculate, correct to 3 significant figures, the probability that more than 2 of these 20 students took more than 57  minutes to complete the test. ( ) 4 It is claimed that the \"top set students\" take less time to complete the exam as the mean finishing time of a random sample of 6 \"top set students\" was 44 minutes. d) Test this claim at the 1% level of significance. ( ) 6"
  },
