      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q13-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Use coordinates with origin A, x horizontal towards B and y upwards: (a) The equations of motion are x = V cos 40◦t, y = V sin 40◦t −1 2gt2. [M1] [A1] The handwritten method first finds the flight time T to B. From V cos 40◦T = 150, substitute V T = 150/ cos 40◦in −20 = V T sin 40◦−4.9T 2. Thus T 2 = (20 + 150 tan 40◦)/4.9. Take the positive root and use V = 150/(T cos 40◦). Eliminate t to get y = x tan 40◦−gx2/(2V 2 cos2 40◦). [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q13-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The limiting trajectory passes through B = (150, −20). [A1] Hence its launch speed is s 9.8(150)2 V = 2 cos2 40◦(20 + 150 tan 40◦) [M1] [A1] = 35.8888768 . . . . At x = 150, height rises as V increases: [A1] Thus the clearance threshold is 35.89 m s−1 [A1] to two decimal places: [A1] To land strictly beyond B, the speed must exceed the unrounded threshold: [A1] (b) With V = 50 and t = 5, [B1] the components are 50 cos 40◦≈38.3022 and 50 sin 40◦−9.8(5) ≈−16.8606. [A1] The magnitude is 41.85 m s−1 , [M1] [A1] and the direction is 23.76◦[A1] below the horizontal, from arctan(16.8606/38.3022). [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
