    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q15-question-1.svg\" alt=\"P B A β α Q R Two particles A and B have masses M and m , respectively. The particles are attached to the ends of a light inextensible string.\" style=\"--madas-width:430.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q15-question-2.svg\" alt=\"The string passes over a small smooth pulley P which is fixed at the top of the cross 1 ∡ ∡ section of a triangular prism QPR , where PQR = arctan 2 and PRQ = arctan 2 . The string lies in the vertical plane which contains the pulley and lines of greatest slope of the inclined planes, PR and PQ , as shown in the figure above. The system is in equilibrium, with the string taut,  A on PQ and B on PR . If the equilibrium is limiting with A about to slip down PQ , show that M = m ( 2 + µ ) , where µ is the coefficient of friction between B and PR .\" style=\"--madas-width:561.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q15-question-3.svg\" alt=\"( 11 ) You may assume that QP is smooth.\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q15-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Putting all the information in a detailed diagram: Let β = arctan(1/2) and α = arctan 2 [B1] [A1] denote the inclinations of QP and RP. [A1] Then 1 2 1 sin β = √ 5, sin α = √ 5, cos α = √ 5. [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-j/q15-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Because QP is smooth, equilibrium of A along it gives T = Mg sin β. [A1] As A is about to slip down QP, B is about to move up RP, so its limiting friction acts down that slope: [A1] The reaction on B is mg cos α, and T = mg sin α + µmg cos α = mg(2 + µ) [M1] [A1] √ . 5 √ Substitute T = Mg/ 5 into the second balance to obtain √ √ Mg/ 5 = (2mg + µmg)/ 5. √ Multiply through by 5/g. Equating the tensions gives M = m(2 + µ) . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 15 is 11 marks.</p>"
